---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---
<BaseLayout title="Yintang — Hall of Impression Point" description="Learn how to locate and stimulate Yintang — the Hall of Impression point. Benefits include anxiety relief, stress reduction, and mental clarity." breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Points", href: "/points" }, { label: "Yintang", href: "/points/yintang-acupressure-point" }]} articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">Yintang — Hall of Impression</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">Yintang, known as the Hall of Impression, is one of the most well-known extra points. Located between the eyebrows, it is a go-to point for calming the mind, relieving anxiety, and quieting a racing mind.</p>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Location</h2>
    <img src="/images/yintang-location.webp" alt="Yintang acupressure point location between the eyebrows" class="w-full max-w-sm mx-auto rounded-xl shadow-sm" />
    <p class="mb-3">Yintang is located on the forehead, midway between the inner ends of the eyebrows (glabella), directly above the nose. It is a well-known extra point and not part of a specific meridian.</p>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Benefits &amp; Uses</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Anxiety and stress:</strong> Yintang is one of the most effective points for calming nervousness and anxious thoughts.</li>
      <li><strong>Insomnia:</strong> It quiets a racing mind and helps prepare the body for restful sleep.</li>
      <li><strong>Mental clarity:</strong> Yintang improves concentration and focus, especially after long screen exposure.</li>
      <li><strong>Sinus congestion:</strong> It helps relieve sinus pressure, stuffiness, and frontal headaches.</li>
      <li><strong>Eye strain:</strong> Yintang relieves eye fatigue and tension headaches from prolonged computer or phone use.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">How to Massage Yintang</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Sit comfortably in a quiet space with your eyes closed.</li>
      <li>Place your index or middle fingertip on the Yintang point.</li>
      <li>Apply gentle, steady pressure in a small circular motion.</li>
      <li>Breathe deeply for 1— minutes while maintaining contact.</li>
      <li>Use before bed, meditation, or during stressful moments.</li>
    </ol>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Science &amp; Research</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">A 2019 randomized controlled trial in <em>Journal of Psychiatric Research</em> found that Yintang acupressure significantly reduced anxiety scores in patients with generalized anxiety disorder.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31177051/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1</a></sup> Research in <em>Explore</em> demonstrated that Yintang massage before surgery reduced preoperative anxiety more effectively than standard care alone.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30322889/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2</a></sup></p>
    <p class="mb-3">A 2020 study in <em>Journal of Integrative Medicine</em> found that regular Yintang acupressure improved attention scores and reduced mental fatigue in college students.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32624309/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3</a></sup></p>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Precautions</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Use very gentle pressure ?the area around the eyes and forehead is sensitive.</li>
      <li>Avoid if you have a recent head injury, sinus surgery, or fracture around the eye socket.</li>
      <li>Do not apply pressure if there are skin irritations, pimples, or rashes on the point.</li>
      <li>Stop if you feel dizzy or develop a headache during massage.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="mb-4">Related Points</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">Yintang pairs well with <a href="/points/ht7-shenmen-acupressure-point">HT7 (Shenmen)</a> for anxiety and with <a href="/points/ki1-yongquan-acupressure-point">KI1 (Yongquan)</a> for grounding. For sinus issues, combine with <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point">LI4 (Hegu)</a> and <a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point">GB20 (Fengchi)</a>.</p>
  </section></article>
</BaseLayout>
